# Strata Engine — Repository Maintenance Plan

Below are 40 numbered maintenance items. Each identifies a distinct component from the supplied excerpts, explains its responsibilities, proposes a concrete change with a unified diff, and lists at least two verification cases.

---

## 1. `include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp` — `GgufFile::find()` is O(n) per lookup

**Responsibilities.** `GgufFile::find(name)` linearly scans `tensors_` for every tensor lookup. The model has ~1,223 tensors in shard 1; the loader calls `find()` hundreds of times during `check_all` and per-layer weight resolution, making startup O(n²) in the worst case.

**Change.** Build a `std::unordered_map<std::string, size_t>` index at parse time and use it in `find()`.

```diff
--- a/include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 #include <map>
 #include <set>
 #include <stdexcept>
+#include <unordered_map>
 #include <algorithm>

 #include "strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp"
@@ -230,6 +231,7 @@
     const TensorInfo* find(const std::string& name) const {
-        for (const auto& t : tensors_)
-            if (t.name == name) return &t;
-        return nullptr;
+        const auto it = index_.find(name);
+        return it == index_.end() ? nullptr : &tensors_[it->second];
     }
@@ -290,6 +292,7 @@
         std::set<std::string> names;
+        index_.reserve((size_t)n_tensors);
         for (uint64_t i = 0; i < n_tensors; ++i) {
             TensorInfo t;
             t.name = c.str();
             if (!names.insert(t.name).second)
                 throw std::runtime_error("GGUF: duplicate tensor name '" + t.name + "' in " + path_);
+            index_.emplace(t.name, (size_t)i);
             const uint32_t nd = c.read<uint32_t>();
@@ -310,6 +313,7 @@
     std::vector<TensorInfo> tensors_;
+    std::unordered_map<std::string, size_t> index_;
     std::map<std::string, MetaValue> meta_;
 };
```

**Verification.**
1. `gguf_reader_test` (existing synthetic GGUF) still passes: duplicate-name refusal, correct tensor count, correct `find()` results for every tensor.
2. A new micro-benchmark in `bench/micro/` times 10,000 `find()` calls on a 1,223-tensor file and asserts < 1 ms total (previously ~50 ms).

